Third-Party PPA & APT Source Auditor:

Scans /etc/apt/sources.list and /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources for deprecated, unsigned, or high-risk third-party repositories.

Validates repository GPG keys against modern /etc/apt/keyrings/ standards, warning on legacy, insecure /etc/apt/trusted.gpg additions.

Signature & Provenance Verifier (Sigstore / Cosign / AppImage):

Integrated drag-and-drop verification for standalone binaries, AppImages, and .deb archives checking Cosign signatures, SHA-256 sums, and developer provenance before execution.

Container & Rootless Podman Vulnerability Watchdog:

Taps into local Docker/Podman runtimes to audit running containers against Trivy vulnerability databases, checking for containers running with --privileged, host network binding, or root user flags.

12. Disaster Recovery, Failsafe Snapshots & Safe Rollbacks
Pre-Remediation Snapshots (Btrfs / Snapper / Timeshift):

Automated hooks that take a lightweight read-only snapshot of the root/home subvolume or capture an /etc checkpoint before applying any USG CIS profile, sysctl override, or batch package changes.

Side-by-side "Roll Back System State" button if an applied hardening rule disrupts Wi-Fi, audio, or desktop daemons.

Air-Gapped Emergency Rescue Script:

Automatically generates and updates an emergency rescue shell script (/boot/sentry-rescue.sh) with executable permissions.

If network access or user permissions lock up, dropping into emergency recovery mode or TTY allows executing a single command to flush UFW to default allow, disable AppArmor enforcement, and reset network interfaces.

13. VPN Tunnel & Kill-Switch Sentry
WireGuard / Proton VPN Interface Binding:

Monitors active wg0 or tun0 tunnels; integrates with Netfilter/NFTables to enforce a hard-drop kill-switch preventing DNS or IP leaks if the VPN drops unexpectedly.

Displays active IP, exit country, and cryptographic handshake status directly in the system status pill.

1. Plain-English Syscall & Journal Query Engine ("Ask Sentry")
Instead of forcing you to memorize arcane ausearch syntax or dig through dense journalctl flags, add an integrated search bar wired to Gemini 3.8 Flash. You type questions like:

"Did any background process touch my .ssh/ folder in the last 48 hours?"

"Which applications made outbound connections while the screen was locked last night?"

Gemini translates your question into the exact ausearch -k ... or journalctl -u ... commands, the local daemon runs them against the audit logs, and Gemini summarizes the raw output into a concise timeline with highlighted PIDs.

2. Pre-Execution "Blast Radius" & Dependency Analysis
Before you click "One-Click Mitigate" or kill a suspicious process, Gemini 3.1 Pro maps the operational blast radius. It inspects process ancestry (pstree), active shared libraries, and open file descriptors to answer:

"If I terminate PID 4092 or block this subnet, what else breaks?"

It warns you if killing a rogue subprocess will crash your desktop session, tear down your local audio sink, or disrupt dependent container mounts, suggesting a surgical SIGSTOP or cgroup freeze instead of an indiscriminate kill -9.

3. Natural-Language-to-Native Policy Compiler
Eliminate the need to hand-craft AppArmor profile abstractions, complex UFW port ranges, or Fail2ban regex filters. You describe the rule in plain English, and Gemini 3.8 Flash compiles and validates the exact native syntax:

Prompt: "Only allow my local subnet to connect to port 8080, drop everything else, and limit failed attempts to 3 per minute."

Output: Stages the validated UFW rule set and a matching Fail2ban filter definition into your staging review drawer with syntax verification passed.

4. NextDNS CDN & CNAME Dependency Untangler
When browsing the real-time NextDNS query stream, blocking domains blindly often breaks essential web services due to CNAME cloaking and shared content delivery networks.

When you click "Deny" on an unfamiliar tracking endpoint, Gemini 3.8 Flash inspects the apex domain and known tracker hierarchies.

It alerts you with a quick tooltip before submitting the API call: "Blocking smetrics.service.com will break OAuth login flows on service.com because session verification tokens share that CDN endpoint."

5. Dual-Model "Safety Critic" Gate for Staged Mitigations
Any autonomous or AI-generated remediation command carries a risk of hallucinating destructive flags (like an overly broad chmod -R or a restrictive iptables -F that locks out your SSH/TTY session).

Implement an automated critic loop: whenever Gemini 3.8 Flash drafts an automated fix or systemd drop-in override, the proposed diff is sent to an adversarial Gemini 3.1 Pro prompt configured solely as a Safety Auditor.

It checks the command against a strict rubric (no credential wipes, no lockouts of the primary user, no disruption of core boot targets) and flags potential side effects before the GUI ever surfaces the Polkit authorization prompt.

14. Proton VPN & NextDNS Co-Existence Engine
Three-State Network Mode Selector: Desktop switch eliminating configuration conflicts between VPN tunneling and encrypted DNS:

NextDNS Only: Binds local network interfaces (wlan0/eth0) directly to your NextDNS profile via DoT/DoH; leaves VPN disabled.

Proton VPN Only: Routes all system traffic through Proton VPN with native Proton NetShield DNS resolvers (10.2.0.1).

Co-Existence Mode (Proton + NextDNS): Tunnels all outbound traffic through Proton VPN while routing DNS exclusively to your authenticated NextDNS profile without leaks or lockouts.

Native WireGuard Profile Manager: Directly manages Proton WireGuard connection profiles (.conf), dynamically injecting NextDNS Anycast resolvers (45.90.28.0, 45.90.30.0) and eliminating conflicts caused by proprietary desktop clients.

Dynamic resolvectl Interface Scoping: Configures systemd-resolved per-interface routing via D-Bus instead of overwriting /etc/systemd/resolved.conf on disk:

Assigns the root routing domain (~.) directly to the active tunnel interface (wg0/proton0).

Automatically tears down interface routes and restores local resolver defaults on disconnect, preventing "DNS address not found" post-session deadlocks.

Automated NextDNS Linked IP Synchronization: Background hook monitoring the VPN tunnel handshake. The moment a new egress IP is established, the daemon triggers an automated call to the NextDNS API (POST /profiles/:profile_id/sync), binding your custom filtering rules to the active VPN exit node.

DNS Deadlock & "Rescue DNS" Watchdog: Monitors resolution latency and error states. If queries drop, hang, or return persistent SERVFAIL, SentryLinux auto-flushes DNS caches (resolvectl flush-caches) and offers a one-click tray recovery action to restore connectivity without restarting NetworkManager.

